# Runbook — sync tokens

> This runbook is invoked by Claude Code's `/sync-tokens` slash command (via
> `.claude/skills/sync-tokens/SKILL.md`), by Cursor's always-apply rule at
> `.cursor/rules/agents.mdc` when the user's intent matches, and by any other
> agent pointed here from `AGENTS.md`. The procedure is identical whichever
> agent invokes it.

Regenerate everything derived from the project's canonical design-system files
— `DESIGN.md` for identity and `IMPLEMENT.web.md` for web behaviour — into CSS
custom properties (with media-query overrides), the Tailwind/NativeWind theme,
the Figma variable library, the mode-pinned token sheets, and
`tokens.lock.json`. One direction only, code-led.

Anyone on the team can run this — assume **zero git or Figma-plugin knowledge**.
Everything the user sees must be plain language ("brand primary is now #E8112D in
all four places"), never ids or raw JSON diffs.

Safe to run any number of times. This is a **maintenance procedure**: its output
is a pure function of `DESIGN.md` and `IMPLEMENT.web.md`, so running it twice
does the same thing as running it once, and running it when nothing changed does
nothing.

## One direction, deliberately

`DESIGN.md` is canonical for identity; on a web project `IMPLEMENT.web.md` is
canonical for behaviour. This runbook pushes both outward and **never reads
anything back**.

**In scope:** regenerate CSS custom properties · regenerate the Tailwind/NativeWind
theme · re-push the Figma variable library · refresh the mode-pinned token sheets ·
update `tokens.lock.json`.

**Not in scope, and not a bug:** reading Figma as a source · detecting drift ·
resolving conflicts · three-way merges · anything that asks the operator to choose
between two versions of a value.

**A variable edited directly in Figma will be overwritten by the next run, with no
warning and no record that it existed.** That includes Description, not just
values. Say this out loud whenever a user implies they have been editing in
Figma — it is the one way this runbook can lose someone's work, and it is much
cheaper to say than to recover from.

Someone who has made Figma edits they want to keep has one honest path: read the
values off the Figma sheet, type them into DESIGN.md (identity) or IMPLEMENT.web.md
(behaviour) — descriptions as a trailing `#` comment on the token's YAML line —
then invoke this runbook. Tedious on purpose. Automating it is the v2 work below.

### Where this is going

Reading Figma back needs a reconciliation policy — which side wins, per layer, and
what happens when both moved — and that policy has deliberately not been designed
yet. Until it exists, a two-way sync would be guessing at the moment it matters
most. v2 adds `/design-audit` (read-only drift reporting) first, then reconciliation
on top of it. Read-only comes first so the risky half is never the first half built.

## Step 1 — Locate and validate

1. Find `DESIGN.md`. More than one → ask which project. On a web project also
   find its sibling `IMPLEMENT.web.md`. Missing on a web project → stop and
   report it; the emission below needs both files.
2. **Lint before emitting anything**: `npx --yes @google/design.md lint DESIGN.md`.
   - Any **error** → stop. Report it in plain language and fix the file first.
     Emitting from a broken DESIGN.md propagates the break into four places.
   - **Warnings**: five are expected and allowlisted — one `token-like-ignored` on
     `extensions`, four `orphaned-tokens` on `line-primary`, `state-hover`,
     `state-pressed`, `state-selected`. Reasons are recorded in the plugin's
     `DEFAULTS.md`. Report anything outside those five; don't block on it.
3. **Parse `IMPLEMENT.web.md` as YAML.** A parse error → stop and report it;
   the design.md CLI does not lint this file. Then validate: every base token
   named under `responsive.typography` and `responsive.spacing` must exist in
   DESIGN.md. A dangling reference (e.g. `responsive.spacing.foo` with no
   `spacing.foo` in DESIGN.md) → stop. Emitting from a broken reference writes
   a media query for a variable that has no base value, which is worse than
   not emitting at all.
4. Read `tokens.lock.json` if present and resolve the **active phase**.
   **If the active phase is `frozen: true`, stop.** A frozen library is finished by
   definition. The fix is starting a new phase, not writing to a closed one.
5. No lock file and a Figma file is configured → this project has never had a
   library pushed. Follow `.agents/runbooks/figma-library-push.md` instead
   (Claude Code: `/figma-library-push`); it creates the baseline.

## Step 2 — Emit the code surfaces

Both are generated whole and overwrite in place. Never merge into an existing file
— a generated file with hand edits in it is the problem this design exists to
prevent.

**CSS custom properties** (`styles/tokens.css`), one `--kebab-name` per token on
`:root` from DESIGN.md, plus `.t-<style>` classes per typography entry.
Responsive overrides come from IMPLEMENT.web.md's `responsive` table, wrapped
in a single `@media` block whose threshold is `breakpoints.large`. Screens
reference one name at every width:

```css
:root { --space-lg: 24px; }
@media (min-width: 960px) { :root { --space-lg: 32px; } }
```

Here the base `24px` comes from `spacing.lg` in DESIGN.md; the override `32px`
comes from `responsive.spacing.lg."@large"` in IMPLEMENT.web.md; the threshold
`960px` comes from `breakpoints.large` in IMPLEMENT.web.md. All three are read
live — never hardcoded here.

A token with no matching `responsive.<block>.<name>."@large"` entry gets no
media query. Absence is meaningful: it means the value is deliberately the same
at every width.

**Tailwind / NativeWind theme.** On web the theme maps names to `var(--…)` so the
media query above stays the single place a responsive value is decided. On native
NativeWind consumes the theme directly, and v1 emits **base values only** —
IMPLEMENT.web.md is web-specific; native has no equivalent file, so no
responsive tier is emitted. Remind the user that Metro needs `npx expo start -c`
to pick up config changes.

**`extensions` values must be emitted by this step, not by the CLI.** Motion,
opacity, border and focus geometry live under a custom key that the official
`export` command silently drops. Emit them as custom properties directly.

## Step 3 — Push Figma

Delegate to `.agents/runbooks/figma-library-push.md` and follow it exactly —
collection structure, the Large-mode source (IMPLEMENT.web.md's `responsive`
block), scopes, code syntax, variable descriptions, and the font recipe all
live there and are not restated here.

In short: the Colour collection takes one mode; the Scale collection takes
Small and Large; the Large-mode value of a Scale variable comes from
IMPLEMENT.web.md's `responsive.<block>.<name>."@large"` entry, and the Small
(base) value comes from DESIGN.md. One variable, two modes, two sources.
Update values on existing variables by id from the lock file, create only what
is genuinely new, and archive rather than delete anything that disappeared —
designs may be bound to it and deleting breaks them silently.

Then refresh both mode-pinned token sheets so the read-points still show every
token, and verify with `get_variable_defs` on each sheet node. The verification is
the only proof the push actually landed; a `use_figma` script that fails is atomic,
so a silent failure looks exactly like a success from the outside.

No Figma file configured, or the write path unavailable → emit the code surfaces
anyway, then report Figma as skipped. The code half standing alone is useful; making
it wait on Figma is not.

## Step 4 — Update the lock, report

Rewrite the **active phase's entry** in `tokens.lock.json` — new value snapshot, any
new variable ids, `pushedAt` from `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`. Never touch another
phase's entry, frozen or not.

Then report in plain language: which values changed, which surfaces were written,
what was skipped and why. Add the human-only reminders when relevant — new fonts
must be installed in Figma by a person, and library changes need re-publishing in
the Figma UI before other files see them.

## Guardrails

- **Never write a partial run to Figma.** Compute the full variable set first.
- A corrupted lock file → don't hand-fix it. Re-run `.agents/runbooks/figma-library-push.md`
  (Claude Code: `/figma-library-push`), which rebuilds the baseline from live state.
- If a change resolves something in DESIGN.md's "Still to decide" list, say so. The
  list is only useful if it shrinks when the decision actually gets made.
- Don't offer a `pull` or `bidirectional` mode, even if asked directly. Explain the
  limit and the manual path instead. Half a reconciliation engine is worse than
  none, because it looks like the whole one.
